  "narrative": "The new discovery batch strongly supports the previously accepted large associations in the dominant adenocarcinoma context: KEAP1 loss remains associated with substantially weaker NFE2L2 dependency, SMARCA4 loss with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency, MSI-H with much stronger WRN dependency, and TP53 loss with much weaker USP7 dependency. These effects are far beyond any reasonable 10% relative-difference benchmark and have tight intervals excluding the null, so I accept them as dataset-specific associations while continuing to withhold causal or external-generalization claims. The most informative new discovery is H14: PTEN loss is associated with much stronger STAT3 dependency, with a signed estimate of about 0.27 and a very tight positive interval. This is a large, surprising, and potentially biologically meaningful association, so I accept it provisionally and request independent validation. Validation is warranted because PTEN loss may co-occur with other genomic or cell-state features, and this analysis is unadjusted; an independent check should reduce the chance that this is a local sample artifact, although it will still not establish mechanism. Many other tested hypotheses are null or too small: media serum 10 vs 5 does not meaningfully change KIF18A dependency; KRAS mutation does not meaningfully weaken RIT1 dependency; ARID1A loss does not show the SMARCA4-like collateral SMARCA2 effect; ERBB2 amplification in adenocarcinoma shows no meaningful stronger ERBB2 dependency and the point estimate is actually in the opposite direction. Some results remain unresolved because their intervals are compatible with null and small supportive effects: EGFR amplification in adenocarcinoma, KRAS mutation in adenocarcinoma, and BRCA2 loss as a WRN specificity probe. These are not rejected outright because the upper confidence bounds still allow borderline effects, but they do not currently support scientifically meaningful conclusions. I registered follow-ups to test whether the new PTEN-loss/STAT3 association is robust in adenocarcinoma and whether it persists when TP53 loss is excluded, because TP53 co-inheritance is a plausible competing explanation or modifier.",
