  "narrative": "Both response-deadline/validation items support the existing major dataset-specific associations, so I keep H12 accepted and closed and now accept H94 with closed investigation. For H12, TP53 loss versus TP53 wild-type with dependency_USP7 and direction +1 was positive in discovery (+0.396, 95% CI +0.325 to +0.467) and now independently validated (+0.395, validation-adjusted 95% CI +0.293 to +0.497; n=432 vs 1568). Positive signed estimates under direction +1 correspond to raw higher USP7 dependency scores in TP53-loss lines, meaning weaker USP7 dependency. The magnitude is large relative to the USP7 score scale, and the validation interval remains clearly away from zero; I therefore keep this as a robust association in the supplied lung cancer cell-line CRISPR dataset, not as a causal or externally generalizable claim. For H94, PTEN loss versus PTEN wild-type with dependency_STAT3 inside the KY CRISPR library and direction -1 was positive in discovery (+0.158, 95% CI +0.063 to +0.254) and larger in independent validation (+0.247, validation-adjusted 95% CI +0.077 to +0.417; n=71 vs 460). Positive signed estimates under direction -1 correspond to raw lower STAT3 scores, meaning stronger STAT3 dependency among PTEN-loss lines. The validation lower bound is still meaningfully positive on the STAT3 score scale, and the effect is consistent with the already validated main PTEN-loss effect H14 and other robustness subsets; I therefore accept H94 and close it. The main supported dataset-specific findings are now: SMARCA4 loss is associated with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency (H5 validated; huge effects around +0.78 to +0.80 signed); KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency (H17 validated, while the opposite direction H7 is rejected; effects around +0.80 signed); MSI-high is associated with much stronger WRN dependency (H8 validated; effects around +0.79 to +0.81 signed); PTEN loss is associated with stronger STAT3 dependency (H14 validated; robust across lineage, TP53 and KEAP1 strata, screen quality, adherent culture, batch_a, and now validated KY-library H94); and TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency (H12 validated; robust across library, screen quality, lineage, and several co-alteration strata, including validated KY H77). These are associations in this supplied dataset only; many subset tests are conditional and could in principle be affected by selection or collider structure, although the repeated large effects make technical artifact unlikely. The principal negative findings are that many broad driver or alteration probes show no meaningful association: KRAS-mutation effects on KRAS/RIT1 are near null, BRAF V600E does not support stronger BRAF dependency, EGFR and ERBB2 amplifications do not support stronger EGFR or ERBB2 dependency, MYC amplification does not support stronger DHX9/POLR2A/RPL11/USP7 dependency, ARID1A loss does not support stronger SMARCA2 dependency, NF1 loss does not support stronger KRAS dependency, PTEN loss does not support EGFR or ERBB2 dependency, STK11 loss does not support stronger STAT3 dependency, media serum 10% versus 5% does not affect KIF18A dependency, and PIK3CA mutation among PTEN-wild-type lines remains null for STAT3 after validation. Remaining uncertainty is concentrated in borderline or untested items: EGFR amplification on EGFR dependency is borderline and slightly opposite the hypothesized direction, KRAS mutation on KRAS dependency remains near null, TMED10 and some culture/low-quality-library robustness tests remain unrun, MDM2 MYC/TP53 follow-up is near null and unresolved, and formal lineage-subtype interactions remain incomplete. The most informative next analysis is to test whether the five major associations are similarly present across adenocarcinoma versus non-adenocarcinoma lineage subtype. I therefore registered four new lineage-subtype interaction refinements for SMARCA4/SMARCA2, KEAP1/NFE2L2, MSI-high/WRN, and TP53-loss/USP7, while PTEN/STAT3 already has a near-null lineage interaction H36. Together with already registered H80, H81, H84, and H92, these next analyses will test internal context specificity without implying causality or generalization beyond this dataset."
}

