  "narrative": "H77 is now strongly supported and closed. TP53-loss versus TP53 wild-type in the KY CRISPR library had discovery estimate +0.212, 95% CI +0.051 to +0.373, and independent validation estimate +0.417, validation-adjusted 95% CI +0.210 to +0.624. Under the +1 hypothesis direction, positive signed estimates correspond to raw higher USP7 dependency scores, i.e. weaker USP7 dependency, in TP53-loss lines. The validation estimate is larger than discovery and the validation lower bound remains well above zero, so I keep H77 accepted as a dataset-specific association and close it. This adds library-specific robustness to the main TP53-loss/USP7 finding H12.\n\nH40 is rejected and closed. PIK3CA mutation versus wild type among PTEN-loss wild-type lines for dependency_STAT3 was near zero in discovery, signed estimate -0.0066, 95% CI -0.0289 to +0.0157, and the independent validation was also near zero with an opposite-signed signed estimate -0.0125, validation-adjusted 95% CI -0.0444 to +0.0195. Both intervals include zero and both point estimates are negligible on the STAT3 dependency scale. I therefore do not support a clinically meaningful association of PIK3CA mutation with stronger STAT3 dependency within PTEN-wild-type lines.\n\nH108 is rejected and closed. The discovery interaction between MSI-high status and adherent culture for dependency_WRN was positive signed +0.1046, 95% CI +0.0370 to +0.1722, suggesting a larger MSI-high effect in adherent culture than in the aggregate non-adherent complement. Independent validation did not replicate it: the signed interaction estimate became -0.0258, validation-adjusted 95% CI -0.1333 to +0.0816. The validation point estimate is small and opposite the discovery direction, and the interval crosses zero. The combined evidence does not support a stable or scientifically meaningful adherent-culture modification of the MSI-high/WRN association. I note that the validation interval is not extremely tight and still permits modest negative modification, so rejection means failure to establish a meaningful adherent-culture interaction, not proof of exact homogeneity across all non-adherent contexts.\n\nH38 is rejected and closed. The interaction between PTEN loss and PIK3CA mutation for dependency_STAT3 was essentially null in discovery, signed estimate +0.0013, 95% CI -0.1499 to +0.1524, and the validation result was a small negative signed estimate -0.0626, validation-adjusted 95% CI -0.2523 to +0.1271. Discovery is compatible with moderate imprecision, while validation gives no positive-modification signal. The combined evidence does not support a clinically meaningful PIK3CA-mutation-specific modification of the PTEN-loss/STAT3 association. The PIK3CA subgroup cells are small, so this is best treated as a low-priority negative/unsupported interaction rather than a highly powered exact null.\n\nOverall synthesis after these validations: the durable dataset-specific associations remain SMARCA4 loss with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency, KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, MSI-high with much stronger WRN dependency, PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency, and TP53 loss with weaker USP7 dependency. The newly reassessed KY-library TP53/USP7 result strengthens the USP7 claim in at least one library context. Many context-interaction tests are either negligible or fail replication, including the present H108 adherent-culture WRN interaction and H38 PIK3CA/STAT3 interaction. Canonical driver probes remain largely negative or near-null, although EGFR amplification with EGFR dependency and some low-n interaction tests remain the main sources of residual uncertainty. The most useful next steps are to finish low-priority specificity and negative-control probes, especially TMED10, constructed research-marker probes, and QC or library subsets, while treating EGFR amplification as the highest-priority borderline claim if any further validation is requested.",
