come-specific NFE2L2 association that is not appreciably modified by measured mutation burden. Reject and close.\n\nH171 SMARCA2/SMARCA4-loss interaction with mutation burden was registered with positive direction but returned a small negative signed estimate -0.054, CI -0.097 to -0.011. The nominal negative interval suggests a slight attenuation of the SMARCA4-loss effect in high-burden lines, but the magnitude is below the 0.1 practical benchmark and is not scientifically meaningful enough to accept or to open a reversed claim. Reject and close.\n\nThe new cross-predictor probes are mostly null and support outcome specificity. H172 NF1 loss has no meaningful STAT3 effect: signed estimate -0.008, CI -0.041 to +0.025. H173 MYC amplification has no meaningful NFE2L2 effect and the sign is opposite to the registered negative direction: signed estimate +0.022, CI -0.022 to +0.065. H174 PTEN loss has only a small near-zero NFE2L2 association: signed estimate -0.048, CI -0.096 to +0.001; even the borderline point estimate is below the practical benchmark, so it does not justify acceptance. H175 KEAP1 loss has no meaningful WRN effect: signed estimate +0.007, CI -0.023 to +0.037. H176 KEAP1 loss has no meaningful STAT3 effect: signed estimate +0.016, CI -0.012 to +0.044. All are rejected and closed. These negative cross-outcome results are reassuring: KEAP1 loss is strongly associated with NFE2L2 dependency in this dataset but not with WRN or STAT3, and PTEN loss remains most specific to STAT3 among the tested outcomes.\n\nThe arbitrary research-marker controls are informative negative controls. H177 marker E with SMARCA2 is null: signed estimate -0.006, CI -0.038 to +0.026. H178 marker D with WRN is null: signed estimate -0.012, CI -0.035 to +0.011. Because these are unassigned binary markers, their lack of association reduces concern that the very large SMARCA2 and WRN effects are artifacts of arbitrary marker-positive groups or broad assay confounding. It does not prove causality or external generalizability, but it supports the internal specificity of the observed dataset associations. Reject the positive marker-association claims and close.\n\nMethodologically, the exposures and eligibility/subgroup conditions are measured predictors or technical/genomic filters, not outcome-derived features, so no direct outcome leakage is apparent. Multiplicity remains a concern: many interaction and specificity probes were run, and I therefore accepted only the unresolved H168 validation candidate where the point estimate was large and the remaining ambiguity was scientifically consequential. For the null or small nominal results, the intervals exclude or fail to reach the practical benchmark, so I closed them. Independent validation of H168 is warranted because it is the only new result with a large effect estimate but an interval that does not clearly establish a meaningful interaction.",
