  "narrative": "Final synthesis for iteration 14.\n\nResponse deadline assessed: H168 tested whether high mutation burden strengthens the association between TP53 loss and weaker USP7 dependency. The discovery interaction estimate was +0.209 (95% CI +0.059 to +0.359), but independent validation did not replicate it: the signed validation estimate was only +0.031 with a wide independent interval from -0.211 to +0.273. The validation point estimate is small, imprecise, and compatible with no modifier or an opposite-direction modifier. Therefore the high mutation-burden context does not credibly amplify the TP53-loss USP7 association in this dataset. H168 remains reject/closed.\n\nSupported dataset-specific associations. The accepted evidence supports five large, outcome-specific dependency-score associations. First, SMARCA4 loss is associated with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency: discovery +0.784 (CI +0.765 to +0.803), validation +0.800 (independent interval +0.768 to +0.832). Second, KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, i.e. higher/less negative dependency scores: accepted signed effect +0.796 (CI +0.776 to +0.816), validation +0.800 (independent interval +0.772 to +0.829). Third, MSI-high status is associated with much stronger WRN dependency: discovery +0.790 (CI +0.760 to +0.819), validation +0.807 (independent interval +0.759 to +0.855). Fourth, PTEN loss is associated with stronger STAT3 dependency: discovery +0.267 (CI +0.215 to +0.319), validation +0.310 (independent interval +0.231 to +0.388). Fifth, TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency: discovery +0.396 (CI +0.325 to +0.467), validation +0.395 (independent interval +0.293 to +0.497). These effects are far larger than the approximate 10% relative-difference benchmark for the relevant continuous dependency outcomes, and their discovery intervals are narrow and far from null. The many accepted stratified claims indicate internal consistency across measured contexts, not independent generalization or causality.\n\nNegative and nonreplicated findings. Receptor-driver hypotheses are not supported: KRAS mutation does not credibly increase KRAS dependency, BRAF V600E does not credibly increase BRAF dependency, EGFR amplification fails to support EGFR dependency and its validation did not recover a positive signal, and ERBB2 amplification is null for ERBB2 dependency. Broad cross-driver, cross-pathway, and marker-based probes are likewise unsupported or small: MYC amplification does not credibly modify DHX9, POLR2A, RPL11, USP7, NFE2L2, MDM2, or WRN dependency in a meaningful way; arbitrary markers D, E, and F are null for outcomes where they were tested; RB1, NF1, SMAD4, ARID1A, media serum, and most TMED10 associations do not show accepted large effects. Interaction results are generally null or nonreplicated: the mutation-burden modifier of TP53-loss USP7 dependency (H168) failed validation, the culture-type modifier of MSI-high WRN dependency (H108) failed validation, and the tested PIK3CA, MYC, PTEN co-loss, lineage, screen-quality, EMT, and mutation-burden modifier probes generally do not establish scope-narrowing context dependence.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty. Several active probes remain untested: H166 low mutation-burden SMARCA2 context, H180 marker E and USP7 specificity, H185-H188 cross-controls, H189 EMT modification of PTEN-loss STAT3 dependency, H190 proliferation-rate modification of MSI-high WRN dependency, H194 PIK3CA and STAT3 dependency in TP53-wildtype lines, and H198 TP53 loss and SMARCA2 specificity. Deferred receptor and TMED10 probes remain untested but are lower priority because related direct tests were null or unsupported. Generalizability, clinical relevance, and causal mechanism remain uncertain: all findings are associations in this supplied lung-cancer cell-line CRISPR dataset, and technical or population-specific factors could contribute. The current evidence supports internal dataset-specific structure, not general biological causation.\n\nMost informative next analysis. The highest-value next step is to run the already registered active specificity and context probes before proposing broad new hypotheses: prioritize H166, H180, H185-H188, H194, H189, H190, and H198. If they are null or small, they strengthen the outcome-specificity and context robustness of the accepted large associations. Only a new finding with a point estimate exceeding the practical 10% scale and a confidence interval excluding the null and trivial effects would justify accepting a new association, narrowing scope, or spending validation capacity. No new validation was requested; the one remaining voluntary validation request is retained for a future large but ambiguous result.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 14 synthesis complete. H168 reassessed with R145/R156 and remains reject/closed: discovery interaction +0.209 did not replicate; validation +0.031 interval -0.211 to +0.273. Accepted set unchanged and dominated by five large validated/robust associations: SMARCA4 loss -> strong SMARCA2; KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2; MSI-high -> strong WRN; PTEN loss -> stronger STAT3; TP53 loss -> weaker USP7. Negative/nonreplicated: receptor-driver dependencies, most cross-predictor main effects, arbitrary markers, and interaction modifiers including H108/H168. Active unresolved probes: H166, H180, H185-H190, H194, H198. Next prioritize these active specificity/robustness probes. Acceptance requires effect >10% practical scale and precise interval excluding null/trivial; reserve validation for large ambiguous new findings. No new validation requested."
