  "narrative": "No new validation evidence was released in this synthesis and no claim assessments are due. I therefore synthesize the cumulative discovery and validation evidence. The strongest and most defensible conclusions are dataset-specific CRISPR dependency associations, not causal claims. 1. SMARCA4 loss is robustly associated with stronger SMARCA2 dependency. The accepted core estimate H5/R5 is a signed +0.784 (95% interval 0.765 to 0.803), which corresponds to SMARCA4-loss cells having a raw dependency score about 0.78 units lower, i.e. more negative, than SMARCA4-intact cells. Independent validation is nearly identical, H5/R39 +0.800 (validated interval 0.768 to 0.832), and the association replicates across adenocarcinoma, ARID1A status, TP53 status, screen-quality strata, growth-rate strata, aneuploidy strata, stemness strata, CRISPR libraries, mutation-burden strata, and KRAS status. 2. KEAP1 loss is robustly associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency. The accepted reverse-direction claim H17/R7 gives +0.796 (0.776 to 0.816), meaning KEAP1-loss lines have raw NFE2L2 dependency scores about 0.80 units higher, i.e. less negative, than KEAP1-intact lines. Validation H17/R13 is +0.801 (validated interval 0.772 to 0.829). This pattern is stable across adenocarcinoma, STK11, TP53, PTEN, KRAS, screen-quality, growth-rate, mutation-burden, aneuploidy, hypoxia, stemness, CRISPR-library, and batch strata. 3. MSI-high status is robustly associated with stronger WRN dependency. H8/R8 is +0.790 (0.760 to 0.819), and independent validation H8/R52 is +0.807 (validated interval 0.759 to 0.855). The association is internally replicated in adenocarcinoma, TP53, KRAS, screen-quality, growth-rate, mutation-burden, aneuploidy, stemness, batch, CRISPR-library, and omics-QC strata. 4. PTEN loss is robustly associated with stronger STAT3 dependency. H14/R15 is +0.267 (0.215 to 0.319), and validation H14/R26 is +0.310 (validated interval 0.231 to 0.388). This is smaller than the SWI/SNF, KEAP1, and MSI effects but is large relative to the STAT3 score distribution and exceeds the practical threshold. It is replicated across lineage, TP53, PIK3CA/PTEN complement, KRAS, mutation-burden, screen-quality, culture, growth-rate, aneuploidy, stemness, batch, CRISPR-library, and IFN strata. 5. TP53 loss is robustly associated with weaker USP7 dependency. H12/R12 is +0.396 (0.325 to 0.467), meaning TP53-loss lines have raw USP7 dependency scores about 0.40 units less negative, i.e. weaker dependency. Validation H12/R65 is +0.395 (validated interval 0.293 to 0.497). The association replicates across adenocarcinoma, MYC, PTEN, KRAS, KEAP1, mutation-burden, screen-quality, growth-rate, aneuploidy, stemness, batch, CRISPR-library, and omics-QC strata. 6. One supported effect modification emerged: IFN-high context appears to amplify the PTEN-loss STAT3 association. The direct high-IFN result H228/R216 is +0.428 (0.326 to 0.529), the low-IFN complement result H230/R218 is +0.207 (0.148 to 0.266), and the formal interaction H231/R219 is +0.221 (0.104 to 0.338). Because the signed interaction is positive under the registered direction of stronger dependency in IFN-high lines relative to the complement, this supports dataset-specific IFN-associated effect modification. Its lower bound is only slightly above the practical 10 percent benchmark, the analysis uses a dichotomized IFN score, and no independent validation of this interaction is available. I therefore accept it as an internal dataset-specific association with limited confidence, not as a validated mechanistic or generalizable finding. Negative findings are substantial. Most simple driver-expected dependencies are unsupported: KRAS mutation is not associated with KRAS or RIT1 dependency; BRAF V600E is not associated with BRAF, KRAS, EGFR, or ERBB2 dependency; EGFR amplification is not associated with accepted EGFR dependency and validation actually contradicts the discovery direction; ERBB2 amplification is not associated with ERBB2 or TMED10 dependency; MDM2 dependency is not explained by TP53 loss or MYC amplification; RPL11, POLR2A, DHX9, KIF18A, TMED10, and RIT1 dependency show little or no association with the tested genomic or culture predictors. MYC amplification does not support broad associations with DHX9, POLR2A, RPL11, USP7, MDM2, WRN, STAT3, NFE2L2, or TMED10 dependency. The tested research markers D, E, and F are negative where tested, apart from unresolved/untested probes. Many interaction hypotheses are negative or contradicted: EMT-high modification of PTEN-STAT3 was rejected and independently contradicted, culture modification of MSI-WRN was rejected and validated in the opposite direction, mutation-burden modification of TP53-USP7 was rejected and validated near null, PIK3CA interaction or subgroup effects for STAT3 were not supported, and lineage, culture, screen quality, KRAS, PIK3CA, KEAP1, MYC, and aneuploidy modifiers were largely not supported. H189 remains rejected and closed because validation R193 gave -0.0599 with a validated interval -0.2215 to 0.1018, opposite the registered direction and compatible with no effect. Remaining uncertainty is mainly about untested or deferred claims and about external generalizability. The IFN-high PTEN-STAT3 interaction is the most promising novel finding, but it needs independent confirmation and sensitivity analysis using continuous IFN_gamma_signature rather than a single dichotomization. For all claims, causality cannot be inferred: these are associations measured in a lung-derived, largely adherent-cell-line CRISPR dataset, and measured predictors may be confounded by copy number, mutation burden, culture context, screen quality, or unmodeled interactions. The most informative next analysis would be independent external validation of the IFN-high amplification of PTEN-loss STAT3 dependency using continuous IFN_gamma_signature, prespecified thresholds or spline-based interaction tests, covariate adjustment for PTEN loss, mutation burden, TP53 status, lineage, and screen QC, together with external replication of the five core SMARCA4-SMARCA2, KEAP1-NFE2L2, MSI-high-WRN, PTEN-STAT3, and TP53-USP7 associations.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 18 final synthesis. No assessments due and no new validation capacity. Supported internal core set: SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency; KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency; MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency; PTEN loss -> stronger STAT3 dependency; TP53 loss -> weaker USP7 dependency. Supported borderline internal effect modification: IFN-high amplifies PTEN-loss STAT3 dependency. Key rejected or contradicted modifiers: EMT-high PTEN-STAT3, culture MSI-WRN, mutation-burden TP53-USP7, EGFR amplification EGFR-dependency validation, many driver/cross-driver and research-marker probes. Unresolved claims remain untested and do not support conclusions. Next most informative analysis: independent validation and continuous-score/sensitivity analysis of IFN-high PTEN-STAT3 interaction plus external replication of core associations. Do not interpret accepted claims as causal or generallyizable."
}

