  "narrative": "No new independent validation result or response deadline is listed, and independent validation capacity is exhausted, so this synthesis consolidates the currently available discovery and validation evidence without changing validation status. The controller-maintained accepted set remains appropriate because no assessments_due were returned and no validation response deadline requires reassessment. The scientific conclusions are associations in this supplied dataset only; they are not causal claims and do not establish generalizability to other cell-line collections, screens, or populations. The strongest supported findings are five marker-dependency associations whose magnitudes exceed the practical 0.10 benchmark and are internally consistent across many strata. First, SMARCA4 loss is associated with stronger SMARCA2 dependency: accepted signed discovery estimate +0.784 on R5, 95% CI 0.765 to 0.803, with independent validation +0.800 on R39, 95% CI 0.768 to 0.832. Second, KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency: signed discovery +0.796 on R7, CI 0.776 to 0.816, and independent validation +0.801 on R13, CI 0.772 to 0.829. Third, MSI-high status is associated with stronger WRN dependency: signed discovery +0.790 on R8, CI 0.760 to 0.819, with independent validation +0.807 on R52, CI 0.759 to 0.855. Fourth, PTEN loss is associated with stronger STAT3 dependency: signed discovery +0.267 on R15, CI 0.215 to 0.319, and independent validation +0.310 on R26, CI 0.231 to 0.388. Fifth, TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency: signed discovery +0.396 on R12, CI 0.325 to 0.467, and independent validation +0.395 on R65, CI 0.293 to 0.497. The new iteration strengthens technical plausibility of the core set in a higher-Cas9-activity stratum: SMARCA4-loss SMARCA2 effect +0.788 on R268, MSI-high WRN effect +0.776 on R269, PTEN-loss STAT3 effect +0.257 on R270, KEAP1-loss NFE2L2 effect +0.800 on R271, and TP53-loss USP7 effect +0.390 on R272. These high-Cas9 stratum results are not additional independent causal replication, but they reduce the concern that the core associations are simple artifacts of weak editing in this dataset. The only supported effect modification remains high interferon context amplifying PTEN-loss STAT3 dependency. Direct PTEN-loss contrasts persist and are large in moderate-to-high IFN strata: IFN >= 0.5 +0.428 on R216, IFN >= 0.6 +0.452 on R230, IFN >= 0.7 +0.447 on R266, and IFN >= 0.8 +0.436 on R242. Formal interactions are supported at those same moderate-to-high thresholds: +0.221 on R219, +0.243 on R231, +0.222 on R267, and +0.200 on R243. By contrast, the extreme IFN >= 1.0 interaction is unsupported at +0.083 on R246, and the low-IFN tail interaction is unsupported at +0.127 on R248. Direct PTEN-loss effects remain positive but smaller in low-IFN contexts, for example +0.138 on R244 and +0.162 on R247. Thus, if summarized using thresholds, this dataset supports amplification of PTEN-loss STAT3 dependency around IFN thresholds of approximately 0.5 to 0.8, while it does not support robust modification at the extreme high tail or at the tested low tail. The IFN modification is also specific among the tested core effects: interactions for SMARCA4-loss SMARCA2 R254, MSI-high WRN R255 and R261, TP53-loss USP7 R256, and KEAP1-loss NFE2L2 R257 are null or imprecise. Marker specificity is further supported by ARID1A loss being null for SMARCA2 among SMARCA4-wildtype lines on R263, and BRCA2 loss being null for WRN among MSI-negative lines on R265. Major negative findings are scientifically useful. Driver/amplification hypotheses are largely unsupported: KRAS mutation does not support KRAS, RIT1, BRAF, or STAT3 dependency hypotheses; BRAF V600E does not support BRAF, KRAS, EGFR, ERBB2, or RIT1 dependency hypotheses; EGFR amplification is rejected, with discordant behavior between discovery R3 and validation R119; ERBB2 amplification does not support ERBB2 or TMED10 dependency hypotheses. TP53 loss supports USP7 but not MDM2, STAT3, SMARCA2, WRN, or RPL11 dependencies. MYC DNA amplification shows no broad meaningful association across tested dependencies. Most interaction hypotheses are rejected, including lineage subtype, culture type, PIK3CA, KEAP1, MYC amplification, EMT, mutation-burden, ploidy, screen quality, and IFN modification of outcomes other than PTEN-loss STAT3. The constructed research markers D, E, and F are null where tested. The new context tests also clarify boundaries: high hypoxia does not modify PTEN-loss STAT3 interaction R274 or KEAP1-loss NFE2L2 interaction R275, although a direct PTEN-loss STAT3 association persists in hypoxia R273; high MYC mRNA abundance does not modify KEAP1-loss NFE2L2 R276 or SMARCA4-loss SMARCA2 R277. Remaining uncertainty is substantial. First, causal direction is unknown: SMARCA4 loss, KEAP1 loss, MSI-high, PTEN loss, TP53 loss, and IFN contexts are associated with dependency-score differences, but the data do not prove that the marker causes the dependency change or that IFN causes amplification. Second, the IFN conclusion relies on repeated dichotomized thresholds and overlapping subsets; the robust supported range appears to be 0.5 to 0.8, while 1.0 is unsupported, so a continuous or spline model with covariate adjustment would be the most scientifically informative way to avoid threshold artifacts. Third, lower Cas9 activity was not tested. The high-Cas9 acceptances show persistence under favorable editing, but it is still important to test whether the five core associations attenuate, persist, or disappear in a lower-Cas9 stratum. Fourth, the IFN modification has not been tested jointly within high Cas9, which could determine whether the supported amplification is robust across editing-quality context. The most informative next analyses are therefore the registered lower-Cas9 direct probes for the five core associations, plus direct and interaction tests of PTEN-loss STAT3 high-IFN effect modification restricted to high Cas9. If lower-Cas9 effects persist, technical robustness would be stronger; if they attenuate, editing context may matter for the magnitude of the associations. If high-Cas9 IFN interaction persists, the IFN result becomes less likely to be an artifact of editing-quality imbalance, though threshold sensitivity and generalizability would remain unresolved.",
