  "narrative": "No assessments are due, and independent validation capacity remains exhausted, so this step registers additional discovery comparisons and defers already accepted active context claims to free analysis attention. The main supported dataset-specific associations remain the five core marker-dependency links; the most unresolved question is whether the PTEN-loss STAT3 dependency effect is robustly amplified by interferon context across editing-quality strata and whether similar IFN modification is specific to STAT3 among the core outcomes. The new proposals therefore focus on three priorities. First, they map the IFN boundary: direct PTEN-loss STAT3 association in IFN<=0.399 tests whether the association begins to attenuate below the supported approximate 0.4 threshold, while the high-Cas9 IFN>=0.4 interaction tests whether the supported amplification pattern extends to lower IFN threshold within higher Cas9 activity. Second, they test context and marker specificity: direct PTEN-loss STAT3 in low hypoxia checks whether the PTEN-loss STAT3 effect is IFN-specific or more generally context-dependent, and lower-Cas9 IFN interactions for TP53-loss USP7 and SMARCA4-loss SMARCA2 test whether IFN modification is specific to PTEN-loss STAT3. High-Cas9 ARID1A testing among SMARCA4-wildtype lines is a marker-specificity control. Third, media-serum robustness in the larger 10% serum stratum tests whether the strongest core associations persist in a common culture condition. For all signed estimates, acceptance requires an interval excluding zero and a scientifically meaningful magnitude, with roughly 0.10 dependency-score units as the practical benchmark. Null intervals near zero will be treated as non-support for the registered effect, not proof of absence. All conclusions remain associations in this supplied dataset, not causal or generalizable claims.",
